# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log

# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT committed, which is the opposite of the
# usual advice — so please don't "fix" it by adding it back.
#
# Every `resolved` URL in a lockfile records the registry it was generated
# against. Generated behind a private mirror, all several hundred of them point
# at a host nobody outside that network can reach, and `npm install` fails for
# every person who clones the template. A stale lockfile in a template is a
# minor cost; one that can't resolve is a broken repo.
#
# In an application repo, commit the lockfile — reproducible installs are worth
# more there. Just generate it against the public registry:
#     npm install --registry https://registry.npmjs.org
# and check that `grep -c registry.npmjs.org package-lock.json` accounts for
# every `resolved` line before committing.
package-lock.json

# A repo-level .npmrc is how a private registry gets baked in unnoticed, and it
# is a common place for an auth token to end up.
.npmrc

# Build output
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Local dev logs (dev.sh writes these)
*.log

